## QGroupBox

Style the frame using the box model, reserving top space so the title isn't clipped, then position the title via the `::title` subcontrol anchored to the margin:

```css
QGroupBox {
    background-color: qlineargradient(x1: 0, y1: 0, x2: 0, y2: 1,
                                      stop: 0 #E0E0E0, stop: 1 #FFFFFF);
    border: 2px solid gray;
    border-radius: 5px;
    margin-top: 1ex; /* leave space at the top for the title */
}
QGroupBox::title {
    subcontrol-origin: margin;
    subcontrol-position: top center;
    padding: 0 3px;
    background-color: qlineargradient(x1: 0, y1: 0, x2: 0, y2: 1,
                                      stop: 0 #FF0ECE, stop: 1 #FFFFFF);
}
```

A checkable box is handled through `::indicator`, styled just like a checkbox (set `width`/`height` of 13px and per-state `image:` urls).

## QLabel

The docs group it with frame-like widgets, so it inherits the box model treatment (`border`, `border-radius`, `padding`, `background-image`):

```css
QFrame, QLabel, QToolTip {
    border: 2px solid green;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 2px;
    background-image: url(images/welcome.png);
}
```

`QToolTip` is described as customized "exactly like a QLabel", with the addition that `opacity` can be set on supporting platforms.

## QPushButton

A bare `background-color` gives a "boring, flat button with no borders" — because no `border-width`/`border-style` was declared, you get a zero-width `none` border. Add explicit border properties, radius, padding, and a minimum width:

```css
QPushButton {
    border: 2px solid #8f8f91;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-color: qlineargradient(x1: 0, y1: 0, x2: 0, y2: 1,
                                      stop: 0 #f6f7fa, stop: 1 #dadbde);
    min-width: 80px;
}
QPushButton:pressed {
    background-color: qlineargradient(x1: 0, y1: 0, x2: 0, y2: 1,
                                      stop: 0 #dadbde, stop: 1 #f6f7fa);
}
QPushButton:flat { border: none; }
QPushButton:default { border-color: navy; }
```

Additional states/subcontrols worth using: `:open` (menu dropped down), `:checked` for checkable buttons, and `::menu-indicator` for the arrow:

```css
QPushButton::menu-indicator {
    image: url(menu_indicator.png);
    subcontrol-origin: padding;
    subcontrol-position: bottom right;
}
```

For an image-based button, prefer `border-image` over `background-image`: the latter "has a number of drawbacks" — it can hide behind the decoration and stretches/tiles on resize. `border-image` stays visible and, with slice values such as `3 10 3 10`, keeps the border pixels fixed while the middle stretches:

```css
QPushButton {
    color: grey;
    border-image: url(/home/kamlie/code/button.png) 3 10 3 10;
    border-top: 3px transparent;
    border-bottom: 3px transparent;
    border-right: 10px transparent;
    border-left: 10px transparent;
}
```

## QStatusBar

Give the bar a background and use the `::item` subcontrol for borders around its entries:

```css
QStatusBar { background: brown; }
QStatusBar::item {
    border: 1px solid red;
    border-radius: 3px;
}
```

Widgets placed inside it are reached with a descendant selector:

```css
QStatusBar QLabel { border: 3px solid white; }
```

## Cross-cutting notes

Selector specificity/order matters across a stylesheet — the example shows general, attribute-based, and descendant rules layered together, with conflict resolution deciding the winner. Also, when you customize one property or subcontrol of compound widgets (e.g. progress bars, scroll bars), the other parts must usually be styled too.

