## QSS for a dark dashboard

Grounded in the rules on the Qt 5.15 "Style Sheet Syntax" page (selectors, pseudo‑states, cascading, inheritance, `qproperty-*`).

```qss
/* ---------- Window & generic surfaces ---------- */
QMainWindow {
    background-color: #1b1e24;
}
QMainWindow > QWidget,
QWidget#centralWidget {
    background-color: #232830;
    color: #e6e6e6;
}

/* ---------- Labels ---------- */
QLabel {
    background: transparent;
    color: #c8ccd4;
    padding: 2px;
}
QLabel#titleLabel {          /* ID selector: needs objectName set */
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #ffffff;
}
QLabel:disabled { color: #6b7280; }

/* ---------- Group boxes ---------- */
QGroupBox {
    border: 1px solid #3a4150;
    border-radius: 6px;
    margin-top: 14px;
    padding: 10px 8px 8px 8px;
    color: #d0d5dd;
}
QGroupBox::title {           /* sub-control, positioned off the margin rect */
    subcontrol-origin: margin;
    subcontrol-position: top left;
    left: 10px;
    padding: 0 4px;
    background-color: #232830;
    color: #8fa3c0;
}

/* Labels inside a group box need an explicit rule: colors do not cascade */
QGroupBox, QGroupBox * {
    color: #d0d5dd;
}

/* ---------- Buttons ---------- */
QPushButton {
    background-color: #2f3640;
    border: 1px solid #454d5a;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 6px 14px;
    color: #e6e6e6;
}
QPushButton:hover   { background-color: #3a4351; }
QPushButton:pressed { background-color: #262c35; }
QPushButton:disabled {
    color: #6b7280;
    border-color: #333a45;
    background-color: #262b33;
}
QPushButton#primaryButton {   /* blue call-to-action */
    background-color: #2d6cdf;
    border-color: #2d6cdf;
    color: #ffffff;
}
QPushButton#primaryButton:hover   { background-color: #3d7cef; }
QPushButton#primaryButton:pressed { background-color: #245cbf; }

/* ---------- Status bar ---------- */
QStatusBar {
    background-color: #161920;
    color: #9aa3af;
    border-top: 1px solid #2a2f39;
}
QStatusBar::item { border: none; }
QStatusBar QLabel { color: #9aa3af; }
```

Ordering matters: with equal specificity the later rule wins, so keep `:hover`/`:pressed` blocks *below* their base rule. If you combine states with an always-matching one such as `:enabled`, either raise specificity (`QPushButton:hover:enabled`) or place it last.

## Keeping the theme consistent with QPalette

Unlike style‑sheet colors, `setPalette()` / `setFont()` do propagate to child widgets, which makes a palette a good baseline before QSS layering.

```cpp
#include <QApplication>
#include <QPalette>
#include <QStyleFactory>

qApp->setStyle(QStyleFactory::create("Fusion"));   // predictable base for dark colors

QPalette p;
p.setColor(QPalette::Window,          QColor("#1b1e24"));
p.setColor(QPalette::WindowText,      QColor("#e6e6e6"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Base,            QColor("#161920"));
p.setColor(QPalette::AlternateBase,   QColor("#232830"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Text,            QColor("#e6e6e6"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Button,          QColor("#2f3640"));
p.setColor(QPalette::ButtonText,      QColor("#e6e6e6"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Highlight,       QColor("#2d6cdf"));
p.setColor(QPalette::HighlightedText, QColor("#ffffff"));
p.setColor(QPalette::ToolTipBase,     QColor("#232830"));
p.setColor(QPalette::ToolTipText,     QColor("#e6e6e6"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Disabled, QPalette::Text,       QColor("#6b7280"));
p.setColor(QPalette::Disabled, QPalette::ButtonText, QColor("#6b7280"));
qApp->setPalette(p);
```

Practical notes drawn from the same page:

- Keep one hex set in a shared header/`QSettings` file and generate both the palette and the QSS from it, so unstyled widgets (dialogs, popups, native-styled pieces) don't fall back to light system colors.
- Widget‑level style sheets beat ancestor and application sheets regardless of selector specificity, so avoid per‑widget `setStyleSheet()` calls that fight the theme.
- `qproperty-*` is handy for non‑QSS properties, e.g. `QPushButton { qproperty-iconSize: 16px 16px; }`, but it is applied only once at polish time — it will not react to `:hover` or `:pressed`.
- For custom widget classes inside C++ namespaces, the type selector uses `--` instead of `::` (e.g. `ns--MyButton`), otherwise it collides with sub‑control syntax.
- Because complex widgets like combo boxes and scroll bars require every sub‑control to be restyled once you touch one, set aside a block of scroll‑bar QSS if your dashboard has scrollable panels.

